From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 19:26:08 +0200
Subject: -Zinline-source-maps: JSR-45 SMAP attributes mapping inlined code to
 its source files
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Squashed from 5 commits, oldest first:

* Add -Xjsr45: JSR-45 SMAP emission for inlined code

Under -Xjsr45, trees inlined from other source files accumulate their
chain of inline call sites as Inlined nodes are dropped at Erasure. The
chains are resolved to synthetic line numbers past the end of the unit's
primary source, emitted in the LineNumberTable and mapped back by a
SourceDebugExtension (JSR-45 SMAP) attribute with two strata: 'Scala'
(definition sites, used by JSR-45-aware debuggers for breakpoints and
stepping) and 'ScalaDebug' (inline call sites in output numbering, so a
consumer can recover the full inline stack of a stack frame).

Ported from the 3.10 stream: BackendUtils.emitSource/emitLines,
source.file.name in visitSource, positionInUltimateSource remapping,
and the older bytecode-test harness API.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude Code), reviewed by hand.

* Emit a ScalaClass stratum naming each synthetic line's defining class

A JSR-45 SMAP names only files and lines, but tooling that wants the
inline method behind a synthetic line needs the TASTy of its top-level
class, and there is no contract from a file name to a class name. The
Inlined node's call trace references exactly that class, so each inline
frame now records it: a fresh chain's head takes the callee class of its
own level, and the chain's last frame - the call site, which lies in the
enclosing expansion - is filled in when the enclosing call appends its
frame. The registry serializes a third stratum, ScalaClass, whose
'files' are binary class names and whose entries map each synthetic line
to its defining class (input line always 1). Call sites in the primary
source keep no class: the classfile itself provides that context.

Ported from the 3.10 stream (positionInUltimateSource remapping).

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude Code), reviewed by hand.

* Emit only the part of a unit's SMAP each class actually uses

Every class of a unit carried the whole unit's SMAP, on the assumption
that the duplication would cost a few hundred bytes. For a unit that
inlines heavily it costs megabytes: compiling Soundness produced 4.5 GB
of SMAP text across 13,222 classfiles, with single classes carrying
2.75 MB each, which exhausted downstream tools — a Scala Native link and
a staged build both died on it.

The backend now records which synthetic lines each class emitted, and
the registry serializes only those. Because a line's call site may
itself be a synthetic line the class never emitted (the inner call of a
nested expansion), the entries are closed over the ScalaDebug chain, so
following the chain still reaches a real line. Mirror classes hold only
forwarders, which carry no line numbers, so they get no SMAP at all.

Ported from the 3.10 stream (BackendUtils.emitSource, source.file.name).

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude Code), reviewed by hand.

* Walk the SMAP call chain iteratively, not recursively

`serialize`'s `include` followed each entry's `call` link by recursion. The walk
is cycle-safe (a line is added to `included` before its call site is followed),
so this is depth, not a loop: a unit that inlines deeply produces a long chain,
and each link costs several stack frames once the `Option.foreach` closure is
counted. Compiling Soundness's `exegesis` module overflowed the stack, though
only intermittently — whether it fell over depended on which build thread ran
it, since the recursion is near the limit rather than past it.

Replacing the recursion with an explicit worklist removes the stack depth
entirely; the set of included lines is unchanged.

* Enable SMAP emission with -Z:inline-source-maps

The -Xjsr45 setting is replaced by the inline-source-maps entry of the shared
-Z registry; nothing else changes.

